{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 20, :comments ["<b>And you shall not wrong a stranger, etc. For you were strangers, etc.</b> Rashi, his memory for a blessing, explained: he too can wrong you — ״for you were strangers, etc.״ And Rabbi Abraham [Ibn Ezra] explained: remember that you were like him. And the Ramban rejected both approaches and explained: for you shall know that I will hear the cry of the poor, just as I heard your cry, etc." "<b>It appears</b> to say according to the well-known introduction, that the souls of the children of Israel are the root of holiness, the children of Abraham, Isaac, and Jacob being the portion of His inheritance, may He be blessed, and all besides them are an evil portion. Therefore they would not be particular about the degradation of a person who is not of them, nor about his oppression, because they would consider him inferior — of the degrees of holiness — and from this the branches of oppression would be born. Therefore, when He commanded them not to oppress them nor to crush them, He stated the reason: that I command you not to wrong him — do not say that he is of the aspect of the root of evil, or since he is imprinted in the aspect of the kelipah, his value is diminished compared to yours. <b>For you were strangers in the land of Egypt</b> — meaning, according to what I explained (Genesis 46:3) ״for a great nation I will make you there״ and in the verse (Exodus 20:2) ״who brought you out of the land of Egypt״ in the portion of Yitro, that the souls of Israel themselves were imprinted in the kelipah. If so, this stranger is like one of you with no difference; therefore you shall not wrong him nor oppress him."], :book :exodus, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 22, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:32:30.929181490Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}